# 📊 Statistics Notes – Correlation & Covariance (Day 21/60)

## 1. Correlation

### Definition

**Correlation** measures the **strength and direction** of the relationship between two variables.

It tells us:

* Whether two variables move together.
* How strong the relationship is.
* Whether the relationship is positive or negative.

### Formula

[
r=\frac{Cov(X,Y)}{\sigma_X \sigma_Y}
]

### Range of Correlation

| Correlation Value | Meaning                      |
| ----------------: | ---------------------------- |
|                +1 | Perfect Positive Correlation |
|                 0 | No Correlation               |
|                -1 | Perfect Negative Correlation |

### Types of Correlation

### Positive Correlation (+)

Both variables increase or decrease together.

**Example:**

* Study Hours ↑ → Exam Marks ↑
* Advertising ↑ → Sales ↑

### Negative Correlation (-)

One variable increases while the other decreases.

**Example:**

* Price ↑ → Demand ↓
* Speed ↑ → Travel Time ↓

### Zero Correlation

No relationship exists between variables.

**Example:**

* Shoe Size and Exam Marks

---

# 2. Covariance

### Definition

**Covariance** measures whether two variables move together.

It tells us only the **direction** of the relationship, not its strength.

### Formula

[
Cov(X,Y)=\frac{\sum (X-\bar X)(Y-\bar Y)}{n-1}
]

### Types of Covariance

### Positive Covariance

Variables move in the same direction.

### Negative Covariance

Variables move in opposite directions.

### Zero Covariance

No relationship between variables.

---

# 3. Correlation vs Covariance

| Correlation                     | Covariance              |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| Measures strength and direction | Measures only direction |
| Range: -1 to +1                 | No fixed range          |
| Unit-free                       | Depends on units        |
| Easy to interpret               | Harder to interpret     |

